readiness — implementation plan
================================

status: IMPLEMENTED (2026-07-21)

infrastructure plan (not a single tool): the server's startup
lifecycle. spec section "server startup and readiness".

the defect
----------

MCP_Server.run (mcp/src/mcp_server.scala) is:

    val mcp_session = MCP_Session.start(options, session_name, ...)
    progress.echo("MCP server ready")
    serve(mcp_session, stdin, System.out, ...)

MCP_Session.start (mcp/src/mcp_session.scala) runs Build.build with
build_heap = true, then Headless.Resources.start_session, then
use_theories for a theory outside the image. Only after all of that
does serve() read its first line. So `initialize` — the mcp handshake,
which the client sends first and which must be cheap — blocks for the
whole build. On a cold heap that is minutes; if Pure/HOL are stale it
is tens of minutes. The client sees a process that accepted stdin and
said nothing, and times out.

The build itself is not the bug and is not being removed. The ordering
is the bug: the json-rpc loop is gated on the prover.

Note the prover genuinely CANNOT start without a built heap —
Headless.Resources.start_session boots from store.session_heaps(...),
so there is nothing to make lazy on the ML side. The only move
available is to stop making the CLIENT wait for it.

backing mechanism
-----------------

  serve() starts first; build + boot move to a background thread
  publishing into a Synchronized readiness state that Handler reads.

  sealed abstract class Readiness
  case class  Not_Ready(progress: String) extends Readiness
  case class  Ready(backend: MCP_Backend) extends Readiness
  case class  Failed(message: String)     extends Readiness

Handler currently takes `backend: MCP_Backend` at construction
(mcp_server.scala:1080) and every builtin's handler_fn takes a
backend. That signature is kept — what changes is who supplies it:
Handler takes a `() => Readiness` and resolves a backend per call, so
the ~20 handler_fn rows in the builtin table need no edit.

state transitions (background thread):

  Not_Ready("building " + session_name)   at construction
  Not_Ready("starting session")           after Build.build returns ok
  Ready(session)                          after start_session (+ any
                                          use_theories) returns
  Failed(msg)                             on error from either

Failed is terminal for the process: no retry loop, no rebuild-on-
demand. Restarting the server is the recovery, and the message says
what failed. (A retry tool is a plausible later addition; it is not in
this plan, because a build that failed once with the same sources
fails again, and the interesting failures — a stale heap from another
Isabelle install, a genuine theory error — need a human anyway.)

method behavior by state
------------------------

  method                       Not_Ready / Failed        Ready
  ---------------------------  ------------------------  ----------
  initialize                   normal reply              normal
  notifications/*              normal (no reply)         normal
  tools/list                   full static builtin list  as today
                               (ML rows omitted — they
                               need the prover; see
                               "ml rows" below)
  resources/templates/list     normal (static)           normal
  resources/list               isabelle://session only   as today
  resources/read               session uri: state text;  as today
                               anything else: isError
  tools/call                   isError, state message    as today

The tool list is not filtered by readiness (spec: hiding relies on
clients honouring list_changed, which many cache through). The
list_changed notification keeps its existing job — runtime
MCP_Tool.declare — and is NOT fired on readiness transitions.

ml rows: tools/list today merges the static builtin table with
backend.ml_tools() rows. Not_Ready has no prover to ask, so the reply
is the builtin table alone. This is the one place where the advertised
set genuinely changes across the transition, and it is the honest
answer: an ML-registered tool the server has never seen cannot be
described. A client that listed early sees the builtins; the ML rows
appear on its next tools/list.

the not-ready tool result:

    session MCP-HOL is not ready: building MCP-HOL. This tool needs
    the prover; retry shortly. Read isabelle://session for status.

and for Failed:

    session MCP-HOL failed to start: <message>. The server cannot
    serve prover-backed tools; restart it after fixing the build.

isabelle://session while Not_Ready reports the state instead of the
Thy_Info listing (which needs a round trip):

    session: MCP-HOL
    dirs: ...
    theory: MCP_Repl
    status: not ready (building MCP-HOL)

implementation steps
--------------------

1. Readiness ADT + Synchronized cell in MCP_Server; split
   MCP_Session.start into the build half and the boot half so the
   background thread can publish a progress string between them.
   No behavior change yet (run still awaits the thread).

2. Handler takes () => Readiness; a `with_backend` helper resolves it
   and short-circuits to the not-ready/failed MCP_Session.Error for
   every tools/call path. resources/read and resources/list get the
   same gate, with isabelle://session special-cased to report state.
   tools/list drops ml_tools() when not ready.

3. run() stops awaiting: start the thread, call serve() immediately.
   The stderr Console_Progress feed is unchanged (it is the operator's
   view, not the client's).

   3a. RE-WIRE list_changed. serve() currently registers the
   notification callback at the top:

       backend.set_changed_handler(what => print_json(...))

   with serve() ahead of backend creation there is no backend to
   register on, and the MCP_Tool.declare notifications this plan
   promises to preserve would silently stop working. The registration
   must move into the Ready transition: the background thread, on
   publishing Ready(session), calls session.set_changed_handler(...).
   Since print_json is a closure defined INSIDE serve() (it holds the
   out-stream lock), serve() must hand that sender to the readiness
   cell — e.g. the cell carries an Option[String => Unit] that serve()
   installs on entry and the Ready publish path picks up, with the
   ordering handled either way (thread wins the race: it finds the
   sender already there; serve wins: it registers on the backend that
   is already Ready). Whichever shape, the test is T8 below.

   3b. SHUTDOWN while not ready. serve()'s finally calls
   backend.stop(). With serve() running during the build there is now
   a real window where stdin closes with no backend to stop and a
   background thread still building or booting a prover. Teardown
   becomes readiness-aware: no-op when Not_Ready/Failed, stop() when
   Ready. The in-flight thread is DETACHED, not interrupted — an
   interrupted Build.build can leave a partial heap, and the process
   is exiting anyway; the ML process it may have spawned is killed by
   the exit. (Interrupting cleanly is a possible refinement, not a
   requirement: nothing outlives the process.) Race to handle: the
   thread may publish Ready after serve() has already torn down, which
   would leak a booted prover — the readiness cell needs a "shutting
   down" latch that the publish path checks, stopping the session it
   just built instead of storing it.

4. tests (below).

5. test_mcp.py: assert the handshake is fast. It currently tolerates
   600s for the FIRST reply, which is exactly the behavior being
   removed. Three changes, and the first two are about not measuring
   the wrong thing:

   - PRE-BUILD THE JAR. scala_build (rebuilding mcp/lib/mcp.jar) runs
     in the isabelle tool launcher BEFORE Main is entered, so it is
     outside serve() and no readiness work can make it fast. The
     "initialize is fast" assertion only holds for a WARM JAR with a
     COLD SESSION HEAP. The spec's fresh-build note (phase 1 results,
     ~line 190) cleans both; this assertion needs the jar left alone.
   - MOVE THE shout ASSERTION. test_mcp.py asserts a tool named
     "shout" is in tools/list. shout is an ML-registered row, and an
     early tools/list returns builtins only (see "ml rows" above), so
     that assertion must run AFTER readiness, not against the first
     listing.
   - keep the generous timeout only for the first prover-backed
     tools/call, which may still wait out a cold build.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

(testing process: [scala unit] cases are munit suites in
mcp_test/src/mcp_handler_tests.scala over Fake_Backend
(mcp_test/src/mcp_testing.scala); [bridge] cases in
mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala extending MCP_Session_Suite; see
plans/README. run: isabelle mcp_test [-b] [-t PATTERN].)

A1. initialize does not depend on the backend.
    T1 [scala unit]: drive Handler with a readiness thunk fixed at
    Not_Ready("building"); assert initialize returns the normal
    capabilities reply, and that the thunk's backend is never
    dereferenced (Fake_Backend variant that throws on any call —
    the strongest form of the claim, and the one that would have
    caught the current ordering bug).

A2. tools/list answers while not ready, with the builtin table.
    T2 [scala unit]: same fixed-Not_Ready handler; assert the reply
    lists exactly MCP_Server.all_builtin_names and no ml rows, and
    that ml_tools() was not called.

A3. a prover-backed tools/call while not ready is an isError result,
    not a json-rpc error, and names the state.
    T3 [scala unit]: assert isError true, the text contains the
    progress string, and the response carries the request id (a
    result, not an error object).

A4. Failed is reported distinctly from Not_Ready.
    T4 [scala unit]: readiness thunk at Failed("boom"); assert the
    tools/call text says failed and carries "boom".

A5. the transition is observable without restarting the handler.
    T5 [scala unit]: a mutable thunk flipped from Not_Ready to
    Ready(Fake_Backend) between two tools/call lines; assert the
    first is isError and the second succeeds. This is the claim that
    Handler holds no cached backend.

A6. isabelle://session reads in both states.
    T6 [scala unit]: Not_Ready -> the status text, no backend call;
    Ready -> today's text.

A7. the real server serves stdin before its session is up.
    T7 [bridge]: this is the acceptance claim and the one no unit test
    reaches — Fake_Backend has no build. Spawn `isabelle mcp_server`
    against a session whose heap has been cleaned (JAR PRE-BUILT, see
    step 5), write initialize immediately, assert a reply well inside
    the build window. Cheapest honest form: MCP-Tools (Pure-based,
    builds in seconds but still non-zero); asserting the reply arrives
    before the build could have finished is fragile — prefer asserting
    the reply arrives AND a tools/call in the same window returns the
    not-ready isError, which is only possible if serve() ran first.
    Belongs in test_mcp.py (step 5) rather than munit if spawning a
    process from a bridge suite proves awkward.

A8. list_changed survives the reorder.
    T8 [bridge]: with the server Ready, declare a tool at runtime
    (MCP_Tool.declare, the existing tools_changed path) and assert a
    notifications/tools/list_changed line reaches the client. This is
    a REGRESSION guard for step 3a — the notification works today and
    the reorder is exactly what would silently break it, so the case
    must exist BEFORE step 3 lands, not after.

A9. closing stdin during the build exits cleanly.
    T9 [bridge or test_mcp.py]: spawn against a cleaned heap, close
    stdin while still Not_Ready, assert the process exits without a
    stack trace and leaves no orphaned prover. Covers step 3b.

open questions
--------------

- should a Failed server exit instead of serving errors forever? Left
  as serve-errors here: an mcp client that spawned the process sees a
  clean explanation on every call, where an exit is just a broken
  pipe. Revisit if it proves confusing in practice.

- the prover-free tools (doc_list, doc_read, list_sessions,
  list_theories, search_sources) could answer during the build — they
  are pure scala over Sessions.Structure/Deps/Store and Doc_Catalog.
  Doing so needs those maps extracted out of the MCP_Session
  constructor (which takes a live Headless.Session) into a cold
  backend the readiness state can hold from the start. Deliberately
  deferred: it is a genuine refactor, and "error until ready" is what
  this plan owes. Recorded in the spec section as the follow-up.

results (2026-07-21)
---------------------

all green except one deliberately-deferred test:

- steps 1-3 (incl. 3a/3b) implemented as designed: mcp_session.scala's
  start() split into build()/boot(); mcp_server.scala gained the
  Readiness ADT and Handler(() => Readiness) (a Handler(backend)
  secondary constructor keeps every existing call site, including every
  prior mcp_test suite, unchanged); serve() takes the thunk plus an
  install_changed_sender/on_shutdown pair so run() can wire list_changed
  and teardown through the Synchronized readiness cell described in 3a/
  3b; run() forks build+boot and serves stdin immediately.
- T1-T6 [scala unit]: MCP_Readiness_Tests in mcp_handler_tests.scala,
  registered in mcp_test_tool.scala. All green, including the
  Throwing_Backend variant of A1 (mcp_testing.scala) that proves
  initialize never touches the backend even once readiness resolves to
  one that would explode on any call.
- T7/A7 and T8/A8: folded into test_mcp.py (step 5) rather than a bridge
  suite, per the plan's own fallback ("if spawning a process from a
  bridge suite proves awkward") — spawning `isabelle mcp_server` IS the
  bridge here. `main()`'s handshake now asserts a fast reply
  (MCP_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT, default 60s) and polls (wait_for_shout) for
  the ML-registered row instead of assuming the old blocking handshake
  already waited; test_repl_builtins()/test_builtin_activation() (the
  MCP-HOL/MCP_Repl path, which can rebuild HOL from scratch) needed the
  same fix (wait_for_ready) — their first tools/call was failing
  Not_Ready immediately without it, which is exactly A7's claim
  demonstrated as a side effect. A8 (list_changed survives the reorder)
  is exercised the same way: "phase 3: registration pushed
  notifications/tools/list_changed" in test_repl_builtins() goes through
  the real run()/serve() background-thread Ready-transition, not just
  the pre-existing Fake_Backend-level "serve wires the changed handler"
  scala-unit test.
- full verification run: `isabelle mcp_test` (unit, incl. the six new
  cases) green; `isabelle mcp_test -b` (bridge, incl. MCP_Session_Suite
  over the split start()) green; `test_mcp.py` against the flatpak
  Isabelle, MCP_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT default, both the MCP-Tools mvp path
  and the MCP-HOL/MCP_Repl path from a cold HOL build — 98/98 assertions
  green (0 failures).
- T9/A9 NOT covered by a dedicated test: closing stdin mid-build exits
  cleanly with no orphaned prover. The mechanism is plausible by
  construction (Command_Line.tool force-exits the process on return
  regardless of the background thread — verified by reading, not by
  test — see CHANGELOG) but unverified against a real cold build with
  stdin closed mid-flight. Left as follow-up, along with the open
  question above (prover-free tools answering during the build).
